QtQml: sort Sequences without subscripting their iterators

std::sort in the libc++ of the macOS 27 SDK (Xcode 27) accesses random
access iterators through operator[] (std::__sift_down), and
QSequentialIterable::RandomAccessIterator, a QTaggedIterator, has none:

  sift_down.h: error: type 'QTaggedIterator<QTaggedIterator<
  QSequentialIterator, void>, std::random_access_iterator_tag>' does not
  provide a subscript operator

QTaggedIterator cannot just grow an operator[]: the QVariantRef it hands
out points at the iterator itself, so one returned for a temporary
iterator would dangle. Sort a copy of the elements instead and write the
result back. The comparison and the result are the same as before.

Upstream has no fix for 6.8: qtdeclarative 6537b2b23b ("Rework the sort
implementation for Sequences", QTBUG-125400, Qt 6.9) removed this code
along with the special Sequence sort - a behavior change, so it is not
backported here.

--- a/qtdeclarative/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4sequenceobject.cpp
+++ b/qtdeclarative/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4sequenceobject.cpp
@@ -102,9 +102,19 @@
 
     QSequentialIterable iterable(p->metaSequence(), p->listType(), p->storagePointer());
     if (iterable.canRandomAccessIterate()) {
-        std::sort(QSequentialIterable::RandomAccessIterator(iterable.mutableBegin()),
-                  QSequentialIterable::RandomAccessIterator(iterable.mutableEnd()),
-                  compare);
+        // std::sort may subscript its iterators (the libc++ of the macOS 27 SDK
+        // does), and QSequentialIterable's have no operator[]: sort a copy of the
+        // elements and write it back.
+        QVariantList values;
+        values.reserve(iterable.size());
+        for (const QVariant &value : std::as_const(iterable))
+            values.append(value);
+        std::sort(values.begin(), values.end(), compare);
+        auto it = iterable.mutableBegin();
+        for (const QVariant &value : std::as_const(values)) {
+            *it = value;
+            ++it;
+        }
     } else {
         qWarning() << "Container has no suitable iterator for sorting";
     }
